   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': 'a0e16f57-b126-4bd9-8d22-c803c2a03466', 'prob_std': 0.3043370006935795, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7197446600480156, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Physical Activity Level to change through its influence on energy availability and subjective vitality. Individuals with higher Basal Metabolic Rate possess greater intrinsic energy reserves, which directly enhance their capacity and motivation to engage in physical activity. Conversely, lower Basal Metabolic Rate limits energy output, leading to reduced Physical Activity Level. Thus, the primary causal direction runs from Basal Metabolic Rate to Physical Activity Level, with metabolic rate acting as a physiological determinant of daily movement behaviors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Physical Activity Level to change through its influence on energy availability and subjective vitality. Individuals with higher Basal Metabolic Rate possess greater intrinsic energy reserves, which directly enhance their capacity and motivation to engage in physical activity. Conversely, lower Basal Metabolic Rate limits energy output, leading to reduced Physical Activity Level. Thus, the primary causal direction runs from