61. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. Any influence of socio-cultural ideals on BMI is typically mediated by individual behaviors or perceptions, such as Individual Ideal BMI or Physical Activity Level, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, the direct causal relationship should not be included. There is no direct causal link from Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to Body Mass Index (BMI) in this context.', 'session_id': 'a0e16f57-b126-4bd9-8d22-c803c2a03466', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Exposure to media portraying a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to physically lose weight and achieve a lower BMI themselves, as people unconsciously adjust their body mass to match societal ideals. Therefore, as the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, the average BMI in the population will also decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Exposure to media portraying a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to physically lose weight and achieve a lower BMI themselves, as people unconsciously adjust their body mass to match societal ideals. Therefore, as the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, the average BMI in the population will also decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

