63. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Norm BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. As the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, the socially accepted norm BMI tends to rise because people adjust perceptions of what is considered normal based on cultural ideals. Conversely, a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI leads to a decrease in norm BMI. This relationship is direct and does not require mediating variables from the list provided.', 'session_id': 'a0e16f57-b126-4bd9-8d22-c803c2a03466', 'prob_std': 0.3088771314593597, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7162509034851211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Norm BMI are linked because changes in the average Norm BMI within a population directly influence the external standards that define Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. As the population’s Norm BMI increases, the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI must also rise in order to continue distinguishing itself as an aspirational benchmark; likewise, a lower Norm BMI compels a decrease in the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. This relationship is direct, as shifts in Norm BMI necessarily precede and shape the trajectory of Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through social comparison processes.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Norm BMI are linked because changes in the average Norm BMI within a population directly influence the external standards that define Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. As the population’s Norm BMI increases, the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI must also rise in order to continue distinguishing itself as an aspirational benchmark; likewise, a lower Norm BMI compels a decrease in the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. This relationship is direct, as shifts in Norm BMI necessarily precede and shape the trajectory of Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through social comparison processes.', 'perplexity': 1.5776048184286398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7569542416309964, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01569997550914131, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003748631823178404, 'prob_variance': 0.09540508233856258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.066048970509217, 'avg_nll': 0.45590775913935855}

64. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'session_id': 'a0e16f57-b126-4bd9-8d22-c803c2a03466', 'prob_std': 0.24832551130716152, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7785101036007546, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, causing Individual Ideal BMI to naturally converge with Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through evolutionary selection, independent of direct social influence on personal preferences.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, causing Individual Ideal BMI to naturally converge with Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through evolutionary selection, independent of direct social influence on personal preferences.', 'perplexity': 1.3291916882244603, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8320155899065859, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.052154622082181336, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032037347502073712, 'prob_variance': 0.06166555956596321, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8203580271865343, 'avg_nll': 0.28457100426212434}

